1. Install the required software

Install the following software before recording WAV files:

Fldigi 4.2.11
Download: https://www.w1hkj.org/files/fldigi/fldigi-4.2.11_setup.exe
VB-CABLE Virtual Audio Device
Download: https://vb-audio.com/Cable/
Extract the downloaded package, run the setup program as Administrator, and restart Windows after installation.
Python dependencies
Install the packages required by the recording script:
pip install -r requirements.txt

VB-CABLE creates two virtual audio devices:

CABLE Input  : playback device
CABLE Output : recording device

The audio generated by Fldigi is sent to CABLE Input and is forwarded internally to CABLE Output, where it can be recorded by the Python script.

2. Configure and check the virtual audio devices

Open the Windows sound-device panel:

Win + R
mmsys.cpl

Check that the following devices are available:

Playback tab:  CABLE Input (VB-Audio Virtual Cable)
Recording tab: CABLE Output (VB-Audio Virtual Cable)

Do not set VB-CABLE as the default Windows playback device, because system sounds may otherwise be recorded together with the Fldigi signal.

Next, identify the device index used by the Python recording script:

python generate_fldigi_wavs.py --list-devices

Locate the entry corresponding to:

CABLE Output (VB-Audio Virtual Cable)

Use the number shown before this device name as the value of --device-id. Do not reuse a device id from another computer.

For example, if the output is:

7  CABLE Output (VB-Audio Virtual Cable)

then use:

--device-id 7

Before starting a long recording session, select a digital mode in Fldigi, start a short transmission, and confirm that the input-level meter for CABLE Output changes in the Windows recording-device panel. If the level remains near zero, the audio-routing configuration is incorrect.

3. Configure Fldigi

Open Fldigi and navigate to:

Configure -> Config Dialog -> Sound Card

On Windows, select the PortAudio driver and configure the devices as follows:

Playback device: CABLE Input (VB-Audio Virtual Cable)
Capture device:  any valid unused input device

The Capture device is not used for the automated WAV-generation workflow because Python records directly from CABLE Output. Do not select CABLE Output as the Fldigi Capture device unless loopback monitoring is specifically required.

Use a carrier frequency of 1500 Hz. The recording script can set the carrier automatically through XML-RPC, but the Fldigi configuration should use the same default value.

Disable any real transceiver, PTT, or rig-control connection unless it is required for another purpose. The automated recording workflow only needs Fldigi to generate audio and send it to the virtual cable.

Start Fldigi with XML-RPC enabled so that the Python script can control the modem mode, carrier frequency, transmission text, and TX/RX state:

"C:\Program Files\fldigi-4.2.11\fldigi.exe" `
  --xmlrpc-server-address 127.0.0.1 `
  --xmlrpc-server-port 7362

The Python script then connects to:

http://127.0.0.1:7362

The final audio path is:

Fldigi transmission
    -> CABLE Input
    -> VB-CABLE internal routing
    -> CABLE Output
    -> Python recording script
    -> WAV file
